<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bridgnorth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bridgnorth is a market town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, built a burh, or fortified town, there in 912 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, who had camped nearby in 895–896. In 1102 Henry I besieged and took the castle of the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-belleme">Robert of Bellême</a> at Bridgnorth, and the town grew on its sandstone ridge above the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridgnorth.jpg" alt="Bridgnorth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gnesener1900 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bridgnorth%27s_High_Town.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
